 "standard_text": "Numbers beyond 10,000: reading and writing numbers in lakhs using the Indian place-value system, comparing and ordering, and place value (summary of NCERT Maths Mela Class 5)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 5 We the Travellers – I worksheet (Maths Mela Chapter 1): lakhs, place value, expanded form, number names and comparing. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "1 lakh = 1,00,000 = 100 thousand.",
 "key_sub": "Indian commas: 3 digits, then groups of 2.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Places: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands, <b>lakhs</b>, ten lakhs.</li><li>3,45,678 is three lakh forty-five thousand six hundred and seventy-eight.</li><li>Compare from the biggest place.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>What is the value of 6 in 2,63,450?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6 is in the ten thousands place</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 60,000</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Writing 1 lakh as 10,000</td><td>1 lakh = 1,00,000.</td></tr><tr><td>Putting commas every 3 digits</td><td>In India: 12,34,567.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How many thousands make a lakh?",
   "a": "100 thousands."
  },
  {
   "q": "How is the Indian system different?",
   "a": "After the thousands, commas come every two digits: lakhs and crores."
  }
 ],
